The design of LegalEase follows a layered architecture. The presentation layer is the web interface where users fill in forms and preview documents. The application layer is the backend server, which handles authentication, workflow, and input validation. The AI layer builds a prompt from a vetted template and the user's answers, sends it to the language model, and checks the output before it is shown. The data layer stores users, templates, documents, and clause explanations, and the export layer converts the final text into PDF or DOCX files. The flow of data begins with the user's input, which is validated and combined with a template to form a prompt. The model then returns a draft, which is checked, explained clause by clause, shown in a preview, edited by the user, and finally exported. The database schema contains four main tables: users, templates, documents, and clauses. The design documentation includes a use case diagram, data flow diagrams, an entity-relationship diagram, and sequence and activity diagrams. Prompt design is treated as an important part of the system. The model is instructed to stay within the scope of the supplied template, to avoid inventing unrelated clauses, to return structured output, and to include a mandatory disclaimer. The user interface consists of a landing page, login and sign-up screens, a document picker, a question wizard, a preview and edit screen, a saved-documents page, and a download page.
